ec864a3\" data-element_type=\"container\" data-e-type=\"container\" data-settings=\"{&quot;background_background&quot;:&quot;classic&quot;}\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-14292cf elementor-widget elementor-widget-heading\" data-id=\"14292cf\" data-element_type=\"widget\" data-e-type=\"widget\" data-widget_type=\"heading.default\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-widget-container\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<h3 class=\"elementor-heading-title elementor-size-default\">Related Conditions<\/h3>\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-666c459 elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor\" data-id=\"666c459\" data-element_type=\"widget\" data-e-type=\"widget\" data-widget_type=\"text-editor.default\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-widget-container\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<p><a href=\"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/shoulder-treatment-dubai\/rotator-cuff-tear-treatment\/\">Rotator Cuff Tear Treatment<\/a> full conservative and surgical overview, <a href=\"\/shoulder-treatment-dubai\/shoulder-impingement-relief\/\">Shoulder Impingement Relief<\/a> bone spur compression treated simultaneously <a href=\"\/shoulder-treatment-dubai\/shoulder-instability-treatment\/\">Shoulder Instability Treatment<\/a> recurrent dislocations Bicep Tendonitis Treatment often coexists with rotator cuff tears\u00a0<\/p>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-83cb982 e-con-full card e-flex wpr-particle-no wpr-jarallax-no wpr-parallax-no wpr-sticky-section-no wpr-column-slider-no wpr-equal-height-no e-con e-child\" data-id=\"83cb982\" data-element_type=\"container\" data-e-type=\"container\" data-settings=\"{&quot;background_background&quot;:&quot;classic&quot;}\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-ffc7e23 elementor-widget elementor-widget-heading\" data-id=\"ffc7e23\" data-element_type=\"widget\" data-e-type=\"widget\" data-widget_type=\"heading.default\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-widget-container\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<h3 class=\"elementor-heading-title elementor-size-default\">Related Procedures<\/h3>\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-85e1bbe elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor\" data-id=\"85e1bbe\" data-element_type=\"widget\" data-e-type=\"widget\" data-widget_type=\"text-editor.default\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-widget-container\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<p>Shoulder Arthroscopy the minimally invasive technique used <a href=\"\/shoulder-treatment-dubai\/shoulder-stabilization-surgery\/\">Shoulder Stabilization Surgery<\/a> for instability cases Subacromial Decompression bone spur removal often done simultaneously <a href=\"\/shoulder-treatment-dubai\/\">Shoulder Treatment Dubai<\/a> comprehensive hub page<\/p>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Rotator Cuff Repair Surgery Dubai: Restore Shoulder Strength &amp; Function Minimally Invasive Arthroscopic Rotator Cuff Repair | Same-Day Surgery | 85-95% Success Rate Suffering from a torn rotator cuff and persistent shoulder pain? Rotator cuff repair surgery performed by an expert rotator cuff surgeon in Dubai can restore full strength and eliminate pain permanently. Dr. Usama Saleh, fellowship-trained at the University of Toronto with 23+ years of experience, specializes in advanced arthroscopic rotator cuff repair through just 3-4 tiny incisions. This minimally invasive shoulder surgery delivers faster recovery, minimal scarring, and outstanding long-term outcomes. Whether your rotator cuff tear is from injury or degeneration, arthroscopic rotator cuff surgery is performed as a same-day outpatient procedure so you return home the same day. Schedule Your Surgical Consultation View Recovery Timeline What Is Rotator Cuff Repair Surgery? Rotator cuff repair surgery is a procedure that reattaches torn rotator cuff tendons back to the bone. The rotator cuff, four muscles and tendons stabilizing your shoulder, can tear from injury, repetitive strain, or degeneration, causing pain, weakness, and functional loss. As a specialist rotator cuff surgeon in Dubai, Dr. Usama performs arthroscopic rotator cuff surgery through 3-4 tiny incisions using a high-definition camera. The rotator cuff repair technique secures the torn tendon to bone with strong suture anchors, allowing biological healing over 3-6 months. This same-day outpatient shoulder rotator cuff surgery replaces the need for large open incisions, hospital stays, or prolonged recovery. When Is Rotator Cuff Repair Surgery Needed? Not every rotator cuff tear requires rotator cuff surgery. Dr. Usama evaluates each patient carefully before recommending surgery. Surgery Typically Recommended When: Conservative treatment failed after 3-6 months \u00b7 Complete full-thickness tears \u00b7 Large\/massive tears (3cm+) \u00b7 Acute tear in active individual \u00b7 Significant weakness affecting daily life \u00b7 Progressive tear enlargement on imaging \u00b7 Persistent pain despite all treatments \u00b7 Occupation or sport requires full overhead strength \u2192 Early surgery = better tissue quality &amp; outcomes Surgery May NOT Be Needed When: Partial-thickness tears responding to therapy \u00b7 Small tears (&lt;1cm) in less active patients \u00b7 First attempt, conservative care should be tried \u00b7 Very chronic tears with severe fatty infiltration \u00b7 Significant medical conditions making surgery high-risk \u00b7 Symptoms tolerable and function acceptable at current activity level \u2192 Dr. Usama always recommends conservative first Evaluation covers: tear size and location on MRI, muscle quality and fatty infiltration (Goutallier grading), physical strength and ROM testing, your activity goals and occupation, response to previous treatments, smoking status, and overall health. What Causes Rotator Cuff Tears? Understanding what causes rotator cuff tears helps guide both treatment and prevention strategies. 4-6 Weeks Before Surgical consultation \u00b7 MRI review \u00b7 Procedure explained \u00b7 Pre-op instructions issued \u00b7 what to expect rotator cuff surgery discussed 3-4 Weeks Before Medical clearance \u00b7 Blood work \u00b7 EKG if over 50 \u00b7 Rotator cuff surgery pre op visit with GP \u00b7 Insurance pre-authorization 2-4 Weeks Before Stop blood thinners (aspirin, ibuprofen, naproxen) \u00b7 Stop herbal supplements \u00b7 STOP SMOKING, doubles re-tear rate \u00b7 Pre-hab PT if indicated Day Of Rotator Cuff Surgery Arrive 1.5-2hrs early \u00b7 Nil by mouth from midnight \u00b7 Bring Emirates ID + insurance card \u00b7 Designated driver mandatory, no exceptions How Is Rotator Cuff Repair Surgery Performed? Understanding how is rotator cuff surgery performed reduces anxiety and sets realistic expectations. Dr. Usama performs arthroscopic shoulder surgery rotator cuff repair in 95%+ of cases, through 3-4 tiny incisions with high-definition visualization. Step 1 &#8211; Anaesthesia &amp; Positioning (15-20 min) General anaesthesia + interscalene nerve block numbing shoulder 12-18hrs post-op. Beach-chair or lateral positioning. Surgical site marked and sterilized. Step 2 &#8211; Portal Creation &amp; Diagnostic Arthroscopy (10-15 min) 3-4 small incisions created. Arthroscope inserted for systematic HD inspection of the rotator cuff tear, bicep tendon, labrum, and cartilage surfaces. Confirms surgical plan. Step 3 &#8211; Joint Preparation (15-20 min) Inflamed bursa removed. Bone spurs shaved (subacromial decompression). Frayed tear edges debrided. Bone footprint roughened, the bleeding surface is critical for suture anchor rotator cuff repair biological healing. Step 4 &#8211; Rotator Cuff Repair Technique (30-45 min) Tendon mobilized. Suture anchors inserted into bone (2-8 anchors depending on tear size). High-strength sutures passed through tendon and tied using arthroscopic knot-tying. Single-row or double-row rotator cuff repair technique used based on tear. Step 5 &#8211; Associated Pathology (10-20 min if needed) Bicep tendon treated (tenotomy or tenodesis). Labral tears repaired. AC joint arthritis treated if indicated. All pathology addressed in one arthroscopic rotator cuff surgery session. Step 6 &#8211; Closure (10 min) Final inspection confirms secure repair and full passive motion. Incisions closed with absorbable sutures or surgical glue. Waterproof dressings applied. Arm placed in immobilisation sling for home. Small\u2013Medium Tears 60\u201390 minutes Large\u2013Massive Tears 90\u2013120 minutes Complex \/ Revision 120\u2013150 minutes Benefits of Rotator Cuff Repair Surgery Pain Relief 85-90% of patients achieve significant or complete elimination of shoulder pain. Sleep disruption from chronic pain resolves as the repaired tendon heals back to bone. Most patients report life-changing pain relief by 4-6 months. Strength Restoration Arthroscopic rotator cuff repair restores 85-100% of normal shoulder strength depending on tear size and tissue quality. Regain the ability to lift, carry, and reach overhead for work and sport. Return to Work &amp; Sport For patients asking whether rotator cuff surgery is worth it: manual laborers return to full work, athletes return to competition, everyday patients return to activities they love. Rotator cuff surgery athletes achieve 85-95% return-to-sport rates. Prevention of Progressive Damage Untreated tears enlarge over time, developing irreversible muscle atrophy and fatty infiltration. Early rotator cuff tear surgery stops this destructive progression before it becomes irreparable. Small Tears (&lt;1cm) 90-95% success Medium Tears (1-3cm) 85-90% success Large Tears (3-5cm) 75-85% success Massive Tears (5cm+) 65-80% success Risks &amp; Possible Complications Dr. Usama believes in full transparency about rotator cuff surgery risks and complications. Overall serious complication rate is under 3-5% for arthroscopic repairs by experienced surgeons. Recovery Timeline Overview: Rotator Cuff Repair Failure Rate (5-20%) Tendon may fail to heal<\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":0,"parent":2373,"menu_order":0,"comment_status":"closed","ping_status":"closed","template":"","meta":{"_acf_changed":false,"footnotes":""},"class_list":["post-9495","page","type-page","status-publish","hentry"],"acf":[],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages\/9495","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/page"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/users\/1"}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/comments?post=9495"}],"version-history":[{"count":14,"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages\/9495\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":10098,"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages\/9495\/revisions\/10098"}],"up":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages\/2373"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=9495"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}